BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Asian Culture Center
DTSTART:20261016T160000Z
DTEND:20261016T170000Z
LOCATION:Asian Culture Center (807 E 10th street\, Bloomington\, IN)
SUMMARY:Who are Asian Americans and Pacific Islanders? (WAAPI) A Lunch Di
 scussion On "[GI5.1][GI5.2]The Mamdani Effect & The Midterms"
DESCRIPTION:How do global political labels impact our national/local comm
 unities during election season\, and vice versa? This month\, WAAPI explo
 res the "Mamdani effect"\, a particular case of how the political categor
 ization of cultural and religious identities shapes public perception. We
 'll discuss "representation": while Mamdani gives important visibility to
  South Asian and Muslim voices\, that same spotlight has unfortunately le
 d to a rise in Islamophobia and anti-Asian hate. We will also look at "ex
 pectations": while many South Asians are known for working in tech\, busi
 ness\, or medicine\, Mamdani breaks that mold as a youthful politician. J
 oin us for an open\, student-led conversation. Free lunch is provided\, a
 nd all students are welcome!  WAAPI is a monthly roundtable discussion by
  and for students interested in learning and discussing issues impacting 
 the Asian American and Pacific Islander (AAPIs) and the general community
 . Lunch provided!&#160\; Free and open to all students.  &#160\;All India
 na University events\, groups\, activities\, and&#160\;educational\, cult
 ural\, and historical observances welcome all IU students and other membe
 rs of the Indiana University community.For more information\, view this e
 vent in BeInvolved (https://beinvolved.indiana.edu/event/12474518).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  How do global political labels impact o
 ur national/local communities during election season\, and vice versa? Th
 is month\, WAAPI explores the "Mamdani effect"\, a particular case of how
  the political categorization of cultural and religious identities shapes
  public perception. We'll discuss "representation": while Mamdani gives i
 mportant visibility to South Asian and Muslim voices\, that same spotligh
 t has unfortunately led to a rise in Islamophobia and anti-Asian hate. We
  will also look at "expectations": while many South Asians are known for 
 working in tech\, business\, or medicine\, Mamdani breaks that mold as a 
 youthful politician. Join us for an open\, student-led conversation. Free
  lunch is provided\, and all students are welcome!<br /><br /></p><br /><
 p>  WAAPI is a monthly roundtable discussion by and for students interest
 ed in learning and discussing issues impacting the Asian American and Pac
 ific Islander (AAPIs) and the general community. Lunch provided!&#160\; F
 ree and open to all students.</p><br /><p>  &#160\;</p><br /><p><span cla
 ss="NormalTextRun SCXW251741694 BCX0"><span class="EOP SCXW111447403 BCX0
 " data-ccp-props="{&quot\;134233117&quot\;:false\,&quot\;134233118&quot\;
 :false\,&quot\;335551550&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335551620&quot\;:0\,&quot\;3355
